[{:seon.task/name "a-cold-node-decodes-in-one-pass"
  :seon.task/plan "Measured 2026-11-10: the first read of a stored index node costs about 610-750 ticks and 2 KB a datom (69 M of a 94 M-tick scan of 113k ``:form/refs`` datoms was cold decode; the second scan 9.7 M); one konserve read of a small branch head costs 11.7k ticks and 33 KB; the fasl decode of the 17 MB reach graph 37 M ticks. It is the floor under a root's boot (`seon.db/preload!` is 1.2 s of every boot and is decode work), every law that reads the program, the first scan in any test process, and each open. Should cost: at most about 100 ticks and 250 B a datom: its share of one zstd frame, five fields read, one object built (a four-field deftype is 8 ticks and 128 B on the Jolt at upstream's head). Suspected by reading, not measured: a node is decoded into generic vectors and the datoms are built from those afterwards; the leaf's array is grown, not made at its known length; keywords are interned again for each datom. First step: one counted measure of a cold scan split into decompress, fasl read and datom construction, which names the largest; no design before that number. Where: `konserve.jolt.fasl-store`, the node read handlers in `datahike.index.persistent-set`, the sorted set's `restore-node`. Proof when built: a cold scan of n datoms costs a stated number per datom, at sizes to 1,000."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}

 {:seon.task/name "a-collections-mark-reads-each-node-once", :seon.task/plan "Measured 2026-11-14 (deps; one gc-storage dry run on an APFS clone of the standing store, 64 branches, cutoff 2026-11-13T12:48Z, the mark root's boot runs in seon.db/retain!): 69,806 node restores, every one a node-cache miss (3.07 GB, 0.51B ticks; 109,251 evictions), and 446,829 konserve.core/get calls (27.4 GB, 5.79B ticks, ~61 KB a call), 106 s wall. The second set is mark-unseen's Jolt arm (src/datahike/index/persistent_set.cljc :197-201): it k/gets every newly marked address to read the out-of-line values a node references, so a branch is decoded twice (walk-addresses restored it) and every leaf once in full through konserve's whole get path. This is what flooded root's node cache at boot (125k of its 140k misses came in its first ten minutes; its steady state is ~3.5 misses/s). Change, in the fork: the mark reads each node once: a branch's references come from the node walk-addresses already restored, a leaf is read with the cheaper read-immutable path, and a node read only for its references never enters the node cache. Second, a design with runtime: a mark proportional to what changed (a node is immutable at its address, so the references a past collection found stay found; only nodes written since need reading), and whether a collection's reads may evict root's working set at all. Done: one read per marked node, counted (reads = marked nodes), and the mark's bytes measured against this run's 30.5 GB. docs/storage.md#collection.", :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-test/a-collections-mark-reads-each-node-once}}]
